<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  AlphaSentinel keep-awake guard: keeps the collector's clock honest.

  Install (or just `make mac-install`, which writes all three units):
    REPO=$HOME/Projects/alpha-sentinel
    mkdir -p ~/Library/LaunchAgents ~/Library/Logs/alpha-sentinel
    sed -e "s|__HOME__|$HOME|g" \
        ops/com.alphasentinel.hold-awake.plist > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.alphasentinel.hold-awake.plist
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.alphasentinel.hold-awake.plist

  The mkdir line is not decoration: on a fresh account ~/Library/LaunchAgents does not
  exist, so the redirection fails with "No such file or directory" and the operator is
  left with no unit installed and a message that reads like a permissions problem.

  Why this exists. launchd intervals do not fire while the machine sleeps, so a
  sleeping host does not slow the collector down, it deletes the time. Measured on
  the deployment Mac mini after one night: cycle spacing of 70-77 minutes against a
  configured 60, and a due pass that had not run 7 minutes after it was owed. Every
  schedule in this project is derived from that interval - a 6-hour cooldown, the
  3-hour cycle-stale window, the 2-day productivity window, the seven-day soak - so
  sleep drift does not merely delay them, it invalidates the soak: seven days on a
  machine that naps for hours is not seven days of evidence.

  Why caffeinate rather than pmset. `pmset -c sleep 0` is the other fix and needs
  sudo plus a policy decision about someone's computer. `caffeinate -s` is a
  per-process assertion held only while this job runs and only while the machine is
  on AC power: unload the unit and the Mac sleeps exactly as it did before, no
  settings changed, nothing to remember to revert. A lid-closed sleep on a laptop is
  still a sleep; this is for a desktop on mains power.

  Verify it is holding (the assertion is owned by the tool, not this label):
    pmset -g assertions | grep -i caffeinate      # "caffeinate asserting forever"
    launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.alphasentinel.hold-awake

  No timeout flag is passed on purpose: `-s` alone asserts indefinitely, which is the
  behaviour verified against `pmset -g assertions` before this unit was written.

  Option flags in this comment carry no leading double hyphen because XML forbids one
  inside a comment.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.alphasentinel.hold-awake</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/caffeinate</string>
    <string>-s</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>30</integer>

  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/alpha-sentinel/hold-awake.err.log</string>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
